{% block summary %}
<p>This is the application profile DCAT-AP Flanders, or DCAT-AP VL for short.  
DCAT-AP VL is based on the European <a href="https://semiceu.github.io/DCAT-AP/releases/3.0.1">DCAT-AP 3.0.1</a> , which is a profile of the W3C <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-3/">DCAT 3</a> standard.  
It focuses on collecting information about generic datasets, distributions, and services made available by a government.  
The datasets and services include both publicly accessible and restricted data and services (developed in and for any technical perspective).  
Bringing all this information together in a catalog makes it possible to increase the discoverability of these datasets and services.</p><p>This application profile is the Flemish generic base profile that aligns with DCAT-AP. Derived profiles can certainly be created for specific domains or communities.  
In the past, DCAT-AP VL was only scoped for Open Data, but because DCAT-AP is now scoped more generically and we want to align the naming with the European nomenclature, the OSLO standard metadata DCAT has been renamed to DCAT-AP VL. The specific Open Data aspects are listed in a separate section.</p>

{% endblock %}

{% block conformance %}
  <p>An application profile is a data exchange specification that introduces additional restrictions for applying vocabulary. 
        Such additional restrictions may include the following elements:</p>
        <p>
        <ul>
        <li>Refinement of terminology (classes and properties) consistent with the semantics from the relevant specifications with a specific purpose as the goal.</li>
        <li>External terminology (classes and properties) used for new/additional terms that do not appear in existing vocabularies.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>

  <p>To comply with this application profile, an implementation requires that:</p>
        <p>
        <ul>
        <li>MUST comply with <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-3/">DCAT's</a> obligations.</li>
        <li>MUST always contain for each class the properties that have at least cardinality 1.</li>
        <li>MAY NOT contain more than 1 instance of properties with 1 as maximum cardinality.</li>
        <li>MAY use terminology (classes and properties) in a way consistent with its semantics (definition, usage, domain, and scope).</li>
        <li>MAG can be extended with classes and properties from other data models (vocabularia) that do not overlap with terminology mentioned in this document or in <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-3/">DCAT,</a> <a href="https://semiceu.github.io/DCAT-AP/releases/3.0.1">DCAT-AP.</a></li>
        <li>APPLIES only the general and specific usage instructions.        </li></ul>
        </p> 

  <div class="region region--no-space-top">
      <h3 class="h3">General usage advice</h3> 
      <ul>
       <li>Ensure stable identifiers for datasets and data services, and where possible for all other listed entities as well.</li>
       <li>Use only absolute URLs. This way, the URL remains identical across all catalog processing operations and there is no loss of quality for the user.</li>
       <li>For temporal information, the time zone is expected to be provided.</li>
       <li>If the geographic description is given as a geometry, it is best to opt for a description with explicit mention of the Coordinate Reference System (CRS) used. </li>
       <li>Descriptive information such as titles and descriptions depend on language. Provide only one value for each language.</li> 
      </ul>
  </div>

  <div>
<h3 class="h3">Information security</h3>
<p>A semantic specification primarily focuses on making agreements about the meaning of terms. This is primarily the purpose of a vocabulary. Application profiles are aimed at agreements in a broad usage context.      
As a result, they include more and specific restrictions that benefit their use.</p><p>One aspect of using the specification is information security. Most concerns regarding information security have no direct impact on an application profile. They are often technical in nature and fall outside semantic agreements such as encrypting data flows. Preferably, these two worlds are separated as much as possible and simply combined in one implementation.</p><p>However, some agreements are influenced by information security. Examples include whether or not a property or the range of a property (an identifier versus a string) is mandatory. It is up to the specification drafters to find the right balance.</p><p>For metadata for services, an important use context is aggregating catalogs coming from different organizations. The goal is to make this aggregation available as a whole to the public. Because entities from one catalog can refer to entities from other catalogs, it is virtually impossible to implement a different information security policy per aggregated catalog. Therefore, this is not included as part of the specification. If desired, this will become an implementation agreement.</p><p>Because the basis of the use case is public sharing of information, it is assumed that the information structure as included in the specification can also be shared in that form.   
Information that has been taken into account and which, for some reason, cannot be made public will therefore not be made mandatory. 
Often, that information will be accessible to the user of the aggregated catalog via another property, via an access page.</p>

  </div>

  <div>
<p/>
<h3 class="h3">Service description and payload description</h3>
<p>If a data service is described, there is usually also the need to provide information about the data accessed via the data service. To clearly define this distinction, the following design decision is followed. Properties directly related to the data service are properties of the data service. Properties related to the accessed data are properties of the connected offered dataset (or datasets). For example, the language property for a data service: the service may be written in English, responding to queries in English, while the data from the dataset offered in that data service is Dutch-language. However, if one wants to indicate that the information accessed is only available in Dutch, this is indicated as a value for the language property of the offered dataset.</p>
  </div>

  <div>
<p/>
<h3 class="h3">Distinction between distribution and data service</h3>
<p>In everyday conversations about datasets and their forms of unlocking, the term distribution is used in a broad sense. A distribution is the term for any form of unlocking. DCAT has chosen to include a service as an independent entity. This creates a tension between everyday terminology and the formal terminology of DCAT. However, even based on the definitions in DCAT, one cannot make a precisely defined distinction between a distribution and a data service.</p><p>To give catalog managers in Flanders a more uniform approach, the following is agreed upon. A distribution will mainly be used to indicate a complete set of data. Something that can be downloaded as a file. A dataset is an abstract entity (a name for a set of data); a distribution of the dataset is a concrete form that the recipient of the dataset can use locally without any other system access. A distribution will therefore not exist without a dataset.</p><p>All other forms will be described as a data service. A data service often has a high degree of independence from a distribution (effective data) of a dataset; it may not even be tied to a single dataset. Or it offers smart access to the data (e.g., via a query interface).</p>
</div>




